{"id":"e8d5e0d6-f203-4ad9-9e48-d745bc1d6d7c","object":"chat.completion","created":1784835203,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError('case must have threshold and calls')\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\\n        raise ValueError('threshold must be a number')\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError('calls must be a list')\\n    accepted_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for i, call in enumerate(calls):\\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\\n        for key in required:\\n            if key not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Missing field {key} in call at index {i}')\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)) or not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(f'losses must be numeric in call {call_id}')\\n        if not isinstance(tokens, int) or tokens < 1:\\n            raise ValueError(f'prediction_tokens must be positive integer in call {call_id}')\\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\\n            raise ValueError(f'parseable must be boolean in call {call_id}')\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        norm_gain = gain / tokens\\n        if parseable and norm_gain >= threshold:\\n            accepted_ids.append(call_id)\\n            diagnostics.append({\\n                'call_id': call_id,\\n                'loss_without_call': loss_without,\\n                'loss_with_call': loss_with,\\n                'prediction_tokens': tokens,\\n                'parseable': True,\\n                'gain': round(gain, 6),\\n                'normalized_gain': round(norm_gain, 6)\\n            })\\n    return {'accepted_call_ids': accepted_ids, 'diagnostics': diagnostics}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to generate Python source code for `solve(case)` that implements the described API call loss filter. The task: given a case with threshold and list of calls, each call has loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable. For each call, compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call. Normalize gain by dividing by prediction_tokens (at least one token, so safe). That gives normalized_gain. Then require parseable == True and normalized_gain >= threshold. If both true, accept the call. Return accepted call IDs in order (stable order from input) and per-call diagnostics: probably we need to output something like list of accepted call IDs and maybe per-call normalized_weights, gain, etc. But the description says: \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" Also \"Compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions\". Possibly we need to output a dict with accepted_call_ids (list) and diagnostics (list of dicts for each accepted call). The specification also mentions \"normalized_weights, stronger_counterfactual, inclusive_threshold, stable_order\". \"normalized_weights\" likely the normalized gain per token. \"stronger_counterfactual\" might be the gain? Or maybe the max? Actually \"stronger_counterfactual\" is defined as: \"For each call subtract loss with the call from loss without it.\" That's the gain. \"inclusive_threshold\" means threshold is inclusive (>=). \"stable_order\" means preserve input order.\n\nAlso \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" So for accepted calls, we need to include fields: call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable (already true), gain (maybe), normalized_gain, etc. But we must decide exact output structure. Looking at typical implementations, might output a dict with \"accepted_call_ids\": [...], and \"diagnostics\": [{\"call_id\": ..., \"gain\": ..., \"normalized_gain\": ..., \"loss_without_call\": ..., \"loss_with_call\": ..., \"prediction_tokens\": ...}]. But the problem says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". Round to what precision? Probably 4 or 6 decimal places as in input. We'll round to 6 decimals to match input floats. Also \"normalized_weights\" appears in the text: \"normalized_weights\" is one of the registered properties. Perhaps we need to output for each accepted call a weight (normalized_gain). So we can have diagnostics list of dicts with \"call_id\", \"normalized_gain\", \"gain\", \"prediction_tokens\", etc.\n\nAlso \"Compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" That might imply we need to compute some aggregate like total gain, etc. But not specified. Possibly just return accepted call IDs and diagnostics.\n\nGiven the fixture payloads, we see cases. We need to produce a function that works on any case.\n\nImplementation: Check that case has 'threshold' (float) and 'calls' (list). For each call, validate required fields: call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens (positive integer), parseable (boolean). If any missing or invalid, raise ValueError. Then compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call. normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens. If parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold, accept. Build list of accepted_call_ids. Build diagnostics list of dicts for each accepted call with at least call_id, gain, normalized_gain (rounded), maybe also loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens. But to keep minimal, we can include all original fields plus computed ones. The problem says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" so we round computed values.\n\nWe'll use round(value, 6) for floats. However, rounding may cause slight issues but okay.\n\nReturn dict: {\"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_ids, \"diagnostics\": diagnostics}. Ensure order preserved.\n\nWe must not mutate case.\n\nNow produce JSON with implementation as a Python string. Escape newlines, quotes, backslashes.\n\nWe'll write the code.\n\nKey points: Use only standard library. No imports besides maybe typing? But not required.\n\nWe'll define solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nWe'll add a check for threshold being a number, calls being list, etc.\n\nWe'll iterate over calls in order.\n\nWe'll raise ValueError for any invalid case (missing fields, wrong types). Use isinstance checks.\n\nWe'll compute rounded values.\n\nWe'll return JSON-serializable.\n\nNow write Python source code as a string. Escape properly.\n\nLet's produce final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1762,"completion_tokens":1502,"total_tokens":3264,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":973},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":98},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}